:"Often cooked with","links":[["Cut of pork","ingredient/cut-of-pork"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Pork ribs","ingredient/pork-ribs"],["Pork rind","ingredient/pork-rind"],["Savoy cabbage","ingredient/savoy-cabbage"],["Tomato paste","ingredient/tomato-paste"],["Ham hock","ingredient/ham-hock"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/0/02/Tonight%27s_stew_thickener_-_a_pig%27s_trotter_%28as_recommended_by_Hugh_Fearnley-Whittingstall%29.jpg/500px-Tonight%27s_stew_thickener_-_a_pig%27s_trotter_%28as_recommended_by_Hugh_Fearnley-Whittingstall%29.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Francis Storr","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tonight's_stew_thickener_-_a_pig's_trotter_(as_recommended_by_Hugh_Fearnley-Whittingstall).jpg"}},"ingredient/peanut-sauce":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/peanut-sauce","n":"Peanut sauce","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 8 dishes across 1 cuisine","intro":[["In Indonesian cuisine, peanut sauce (bumbu kacang) is a sauce made from ground roasted or fried peanuts, widely used in  and many other dishes throughout the world. Specific versions of this sauce include satay sauce (saté sauce), pecel sauce, and sambal kacang, the satay having been adapted globally."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-peanut-sauce"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peanut_sauce"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Cilok","dish/cilok"],["Gado-gado","dish/gado-gado"],["Karedok","dish/karedok"],["Ketoprak (dish)","dish/ketoprak-dish"],["Nasi lengko","dish/nasi-lengko"],["Nasi tempong","dish/nasi-tempong"],["Rojak","dish/rojak"],["Siomay","dish/siomay"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cucumber","ingredient/cucumber"],["Krupuk","ingredient/krupuk"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Tofu","ingredient/tofu"],["Mung bean sprout","ingredient/mung-bean-sprout"],["Tapioca","ingredient/tapioca"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Lontong","ingredient/lontong"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Rice cake","ingredient/rice-cake"],["Tempeh","ingredient/tempeh"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Holy basil","ingredient/holy-basil"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Tahu goreng","ingredient/tahu-goreng"],["Sambal","ingredient/sambal"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/a/a1/Pecel_Solo.JPG/500px-Pecel_Solo.JPG","w":500,"h":442,"by":"Gunawan Kartapranata","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pecel_Solo.JPG"}},"ingredient/papain":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/papain","n":"Papain","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Papain, also known as papaya proteinase I, is a cysteine protease (EC 3.4.22.2) enzyme present in papaya (Carica papaya) and mountain papaya (Vasconcellea cundinamarcensis). It is the namesake member of the papain-like protease family."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-papain"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Papain"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Batak cuisine","cuisine/batak-cuisine",[["Dali ni horbo","dish/dali-ni-horbo"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bromelain","ingredient/bromelain"],["Pineapple","ingredient/pineapple"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/0/06/Papain_cartoon.png/500px-Papain_cartoon.png","w":500,"h":383,"by":"Roadnottaken","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Papain_cartoon.png"}},"ingredient/baby-corn":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/baby-corn","n":"Baby corn","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Baby corn (also known as young corn, cornlettes, child corn  or baby sweetcorn) is a cereal grain taken from corn (maize) harvested early while the stalks are still small and immature. It typically is eaten whole including the cob, in raw, pickled, and cooked forms. Baby corn is common in stir fry dishes."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-baby-corn"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baby_corn"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Lao cuisine","cuisine/lao-cuisine",[["Drunken noodles","dish/drunken-noodles"]]],["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine",[["Drunken noodles","dish/drunken-noodles"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bean sprout","ingredient/bean-sprout"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Fish sauce","ingredient/fish-sauce"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Holy basil","ingredient/holy-basil"],["Oyster sauce","ingredient/oyster-sauce"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Pisum sativum subsp. sativum convar. axiphium","ingredient/pisum-sativum-subsp-sativum-convar-axiphium"],["Shahe fen","ingredient/shahe-fen"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Tofu","ingredient/tofu"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/a/a1/Baby_corn.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Jill Schenck","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baby_corn.jpg"}},"dish/empal-gentong":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/empal-gentong","n":"Empal gentong","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Empal gentong is a spicy Indonesian curry-like beef soup originating in Cirebon, West Java. It is a variety of the Soto cuisine and is similar to gulai which is usually cooked with firewood in a gentong stove (Javanese for clay pot). The ingredients include cuts of beef and cow offal such as intestine, tripes, lungs, etc. cooked with curry-like spices in coconut milk, garlic, chilies, chives (kuchai), and sambal in the form of chili powder. Empal gentong can be eaten with steamed rice, ketupat or lontong. It is originated in Battembat, Tengah Tani, and Cirebon Regency."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-empal-gentong"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Empal_gentong"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":12,"links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Offal","ingredient/offal"],["Shallot","ingredient/shallot"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Tripe","ingredient/tripe"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Indonesian cuisine","links":[["Arem-arem","dish/arem-arem"],["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Asam pedas","dish/asam-pedas"],["Ayam goreng","dish/ayam-goreng"],["Ayam goreng kalasan","dish/ayam-goreng-kalasan"],["Ayam panggang","dish/ayam-panggang"],["Ayam Taliwang","dish/ayam-taliwang"],["Bajigur","dish/bajigur"],["Bakakak hayam","dish/bakakak-hayam"],["Bakpia","dish/bakpia"],["Bakwan","dish/bakwan"],["Bekasang","dish/bekasang"],["Betutu","dish/betutu"],["Bika ambon","dish/bika-ambon"],["Binte biluhuta","dish/binte-biluhuta"],["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"],["Bolu kukus","dish/bolu-kukus"],["Botok","dish/botok"],["Brem","dish/brem"],["Bubur kacang hijau","dish/bubur-kacang-hijau"],["Bubur ketan hitam","dish/bubur